:::style
@page { size: A4 portrait; margin: 22mm 18mm 22mm 18mm; }
@page :first { margin-top: 32mm; }
@page :left { margin-left: 26mm; margin-right: 18mm; }
@page :right { margin-left: 18mm; margin-right: 26mm; }

body { font-family: "Source Serif Pro", Georgia, serif; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.55; color: #1f2528; }
h1, h2, h3 { page-break-after: avoid; font-family: "Source Sans Pro", system-ui, sans-serif; color: #0f172a; }
h1 { font-size: 22pt; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12pt; border-bottom: 1pt solid #cbd5e1; padding-bottom: 6pt; }
h2 { font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 18pt; margin-bottom: 8pt; }

aside { border-inline-start: 4pt solid #b45309; background-color: #fff7ed; padding: 8pt 10pt; margin: 10pt 0; border-radius: 4pt; page-break-inside: avoid; }

table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; page-break-inside: avoid; margin: 12pt 0; }
th, td { border: 0.5pt solid #94a3b8; padding: 4pt 8pt; vertical-align: top; }
th { background-color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 600; }

figure { margin: 14pt 0; padding: 10pt; border: 0.5pt solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 6pt; page-break-inside: avoid; }
figcaption { margin-top: 6pt; font-size: 9.5pt; color: #64748b; }

.pagebreak { page-break-before: always; border: 0; margin: 0; }
.cover { text-align: center; padding-top: 60mm; page-break-after: always; }
.cover h1 { font-size: 36pt; border: 0; margin-bottom: 8pt; }
.cover .subtitle { font-size: 14pt; color: #475569; }
::: style

::::section {#cover .cover}
# Pagination, Page Layout, and Box Model {#cover-title}

[A demonstration of NODX print-ready typography]{.subtitle}

:::pagebreak {#after-cover}
::: pagebreak
:::: section

::::section {#page-rules}
## Page Rules {#page-rules-heading}

The block above declares `@page`, `@page :first`, `@page :left`, and
`@page :right`. The renderer applies them only when the host generates a paged
output (PDF, print preview). Screens display the block as ordinary content
since `@page` is ignored outside paged media.

| Rule | Property | Default | Allowed values |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Page size | `size` | A4 portrait | A3, A4, A5, B5, Letter, Legal, Tabloid + `portrait` or `landscape` |
| Margins | `margin` | 18mm | length values: `mm`, `cm`, `in`, `pt`, `px` |
| Bleed | `bleed` | 0 | length values |
| Marks | `marks` | none | `crop`, `cross`, `crop cross` |

:::note {type="info"}
Margins accept the four-value shorthand (`top right bottom left`) or the
longhand `margin-top`, `margin-right`, `margin-bottom`, `margin-left`. Logical
properties `margin-block-start` and `margin-inline-start` are RECOMMENDED for
i18n.
::: note
:::: section

::::section {#box-model}
## Box Model {#box-model-heading}

NODS exposes a constrained box model. Each block can declare:

- `padding` (and per-side longhand)
- `border` (with `border-radius`, `border-color`, `border-style`)
- `margin` (and per-side longhand)
- `width`, `min-width`, `max-width`, `height`, `min-height`, `max-height`
- `gap` for grid/flex containers (Style Profile only)

:::figure {#fig-box}
:::image {src="assets/box-model.svg" alt="NODX box model: content, padding, border, margin"}
::: image
:::caption
NODX inherits the CSS box model with logical-direction synonyms.
::: caption
::: figure
:::: section

::::section {#breaks}
## Page Breaks and Avoidance {#breaks-heading}

Authors control pagination through:

1. `:::pagebreak` to force a break.
2. CSS `page-break-before: always` on a class or element.
3. `page-break-inside: avoid` to keep tables, figures, and callouts together.
4. `orphans` and `widows` to prevent stranded lines.

:::pagebreak
::: pagebreak

### Even/Odd Pages {#even-odd}

The cover used `page-break-after: always` so the next section begins on a new
page. With `@page :left` and `@page :right` rules above, even pages get a
larger left margin and odd pages get a larger right margin, suitable for
booklet binding.
:::: section

::::section {#units}
## Length Units {#units-heading}

| Category | Units | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Absolute | `pt`, `mm`, `cm`, `in`, `pc`, `px` | `1in = 72pt = 25.4mm = 96px` |
| Relative | `em`, `rem`, `%`, `ex`, `ch` | Resolve against context |
| Viewport | `vw`, `vh`, `vmin`, `vmax` | Screen-only, ignored on print |

:::note {type="warning"}
Mixing absolute and relative units in print SHOULD be avoided. Prefer `pt` and
`mm` for print rules; reserve `rem`/`em` for fluid screen layouts.
::: note
:::: section
